{"reference":"Malachi 2","book":"Malachi","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Malachi 2 moves from an indictment of priests to a wider charge of communal faithlessness. The priests are measured against the remembered covenant with Levi: truthful instruction, upright conduct, and teaching that turns people from wrongdoing. Their failure is therefore not presented as private misconduct alone; their distorted instruction causes others to stumble. The chapter then connects faithlessness within the community, marriage, worship, and complaints about justice through a series of accusations and audience questions.","verse_refs":["Malachi 2:4-10","Malachi 2:10-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_002_414835861d43e16f737d0565","reader_path_malachi_002_40845c1d1d43b3016a203866"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions in the Passage","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The repeated objections—such as “Wherefore?” and “Wherein have we wearied him?”—are part of Malachi’s disputation style: assertions are followed by questions and extended answers. This form makes the chapter read as a confrontation with resistance as well as a declaration of judgment. Verses 15–16 are especially difficult in their wording and syntax, so translations differ over such questions as the subject of the statements, offspring, divorce, violence, and the garment imagery. Readers should be careful not to make one translation’s phrasing settle every detail of those verses.","verse_refs":["Malachi 2:10-17","Malachi 2:15-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_002_414835861d43e16f737d0565"]},{"id":"block_3","text":"In the marriage accusation, the wife of one’s youth is called both a companion and a covenant partner, while the Lord is described as witness between the spouses. The chapter also condemns Judah’s marriage to “the daughter of a foreign god,” alongside its language of faithlessness and profaning what is holy. These closely placed accusations show that the passage treats covenant-breaking in communal and marital terms, though several details of the wording remain disputed.","verse_refs":["Malachi 2:10-16","Malachi 2:11-12","Malachi 2:13-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_002_40845c1d1d43b3016a203866","reader_path_malachi_002_414835861d43e16f737d0565"]}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The Closing Complaint","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Verse 17 closes the chapter with the community’s complaint that evildoers are pleasing to the Lord, or with its challenge, “Where is the God of justice?” This question introduces the messenger oracle that follows, so the chapter ends without resolving the complaint on the spot.","verse_refs":["Malachi 2:17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_002_c8674dd11a6c468e57df3cb0"]}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Textual Witnesses","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Substantial Hebrew text from Malachi 2–3 survives in 4QXIIa, and an early Jewish commentary on Malachi also survives among the Qumran texts. These witnesses show that Malachi was copied and interpreted in Jewish communities at an early stage.","verse_refs":["Malachi 2:10-16","Malachi 2:13-14","Malachi 2:8-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_002_40845c1d1d43b3016a203866"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}